  ],
  "text": "Send a photo and it is torn into thousands of pieces that take different routes and arrive out of order. You imagine a line opening between you and the other end. Nothing like that happens. Each piece gets an address and a position, and is then completely on its own. Every junction it reaches looks at the address and picks whichever way looks best right now. Which means two pieces of the same photo can cross different continents. And that was the point. It was designed with no central place to break. Knock out any junction and the pieces just go around it. At the far end they are sorted back into order by the numbers they carry. Anything missing gets asked for again, quietly, before you ever see the picture. Which is why the internet gets slower when it breaks, instead of stopping. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
